-- title:
--   Minimal-level cohomology is free over the local Hecke algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain, complete for its maximal-ideal adic topology and with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, and let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq 2$ and $p\in\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,\mathcal O$. Let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$ (a rank-two $k$-space with an action of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ trivial on the absolute Galois group of some finite extension of $\mathbb Q$) which is absolutely irreducible, i.e. irreducible after base change to $\overline{k}$. Let $S,S_{\min}$ be finite sets of naturals with every element of $S$ prime, $p\in S_{\min}\subseteq S$, such that a prime $q\neq p$ lies in $S_{\min}$ exactly when $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$ (some element of the inertia subgroup at a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $q$ acts non-trivially), and such that for $q\in S_{\min}$, $q\neq p$, every inertia element at $q$ acts on $\bar\rho$, viewed as an adic representation over $k$, with characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$. Let $N\neq 0$ be squarefree, with all its prime divisors in $S$, divisible by every $q\in S_{\min}\setminus\{p\}$, with every prime divisor $\neq p$ of $N$ lying in $S_{\min}$, and such that if $p\mid N$ then $\bar\rho$ is not flat at $p$ in the sense of `GaloisRep.IsFlatAt`. Let $r$ be a natural number, assume that the weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma_0(N)$ are spanned over $\mathbb C$ by the forms with integral $q$-expansion coefficients, and let $\theta\colon\mathbb T=$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) $\to k$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra generated over $\mathbb Z$ by the $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$) and the $U_q$ ($q\mid N$, $q\notin S$) such that for every prime $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\ell$ and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\theta(T_\ell)X+\ell$. Finally let $hc_0$ witness that the operators [`CuspForm.AuxLevel.baseOp N r S 𝒪 g`](def/CuspForm_AuxLevelHeckeModuleBase.html#L9) on $H^1(\Gamma(N),\mathcal O)=$ [`CohCarrier.H1 N ⊤ 𝒪`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L162) commute pairwise, the index $g$ running over the generators $\ell$ prime, $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\neq r$. Then there exist a type $M_0$ with an abelian group structure, a module structure over the local Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) (the localisation of the $\mathcal O$-base-changed Hecke lattice algebra at the prime determined by $\theta$) and over $\mathcal O$, compatibly as a scalar tower, such that $M_0$ is free over [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131), together with an $\mathcal O$-linear isomorphism $e_0$ from $M_0$ onto [`CuspForm.AuxLevel.baseML N r S 𝒪 θ hc₀`](def/CuspForm_AuxLevelHeckeModuleBase.html#L31), the module `ML` of the Hecke datum with carrier $H^1(\Gamma(N),\mathcal O)$, operators `baseOp` and residual character $g\mapsto\theta(T_{\ell_g})$, which for every generator $g$ carries the action of $\pi(T_{\ell_g})$ on $M_0$ to the action of the polynomial variable $X_g$ of `MvPolynomial (Gen N r S) 𝒪` on `baseML`.
--
--   This is the multiplicity-one statement at the minimal level, due to Mazur, Ribet and Wiles, in the shape required by the Taylor–Wiles argument: the cohomology of $\Gamma_0(N)$ localised at the eigensystem $\theta$ of $\bar\rho$ is free over the local Hecke algebra generated by the operators away from $S$, the freeness being transported along an $\mathcal O$-linear isomorphism that matches the polynomial variables $X_g$ with the images of the $T_\ell$. It serves as the level-$N$ input for [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.free_of_linearEquiv_auxLevel_ML`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.free_of_linearEquiv_auxLevel_ML), the corresponding freeness at the auxiliary level involving $r$.
